          "derivation": "Both words in this box are misdrawn, in different degrees. (a) punch 'the beverage': the chart follows the standard 19th-20th c. account — Hindi pāñč (< Skt. páñca) 'five', because the drink had five ingredients (spirit, sugar, lemon, water, spice) — which is what Klein and Watkins print. But this is the one etymology on the page that modern lexicography rejects: the OED and Etymonline record that no drink called *panch is attested in India (or anywhere) before the English word, that English 'punch' is attested before the English were regular traders in the Indies, and that there are phonetic and historical difficulties; the five-ingredient story goes back only to John Fryer's account of 1698. The favoured alternative is a sailors' shortening of puncheon, the c. 70-gallon liquor cask (ME poncion, from OF poinçon), possibly crossed with paunch (older spelling 'paunche pot'). (b) Punjab is not from Sanskrit at all: it is Hindi/Urdu Panjāb, borrowed from Persian panj-āb 'five waters' (the five rivers of the Indus system), i.e. from node #3 of this same chart, not from the Sanskrit box it hangs under; Nourai's own cross-reference ☞Ab 1 shows he knew the second element was Persian āb.",
